comments.body:
A few (hopefully helpful) clarifications about how surrogacy in the U.S. actually works:
- Qualifying as a surrogate isn't easy. In order to pass screening, a surrogate has to have had relatively easy, uncomplicated pregnancies and deliveries and pass detailed medical and psychological evaluation;
- Well-written surrogacy contracts are clear that the surrogate ultimately controls all medical decisions while the child is in her body. While she typically agrees to permit the intended parents to make decisions regarding the baby's health/welfare (including termination when there is a serious abnormality, since it is their child, whom they love and will be responsible for raising), all parties acknowledge that the surrogate's Constitutional rights trump the intended parents' contractual rights;
- Economic coercion is disqualifying: reputable agencies and attorneys do not work with low-income surrogates. The vast majority of surrogates are educated and gainfully employed (and compensated for lost wages if they go on bedrest);
- Intended mothers are not protecting their figures or social schedules: most would give ANYTHING to carry their own child but are medically incapable. Many have suffered multiple miscarriages/stillbirths in their attempts to have a family before pursuing surrogacy as a last resort.
- Query: Why do we celebrate men who beat each other up (risking CTE or worse) for money/entertainment but vilify women who choose to use their bodies to help build families?
